{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 38, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending as a new statute reinstates penalties for carrying small quantities of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth. Under the updated rules, individuals caught with these drugs can opt for a health-focused pathway instead of prosecution—provided they complete a behavioral intervention and engage in a diversion initiative. Still, failure to comply may result in a misdemeanor charge, carrying a maximum sentence of half a year behind bars.\n\n***\n\nThe law also empowers law enforcement to act more decisively in public spaces, particularly near areas like playgrounds or recreational zones, where drug sales will face stiffer consequences. While local governments are encouraged to design their own support systems linking people to care, they are not required to do so. This flexibility has drawn both praise for local autonomy and criticism for potentially uneven outcomes across regions.\n\n***\n\nPortland’s police chief noted that eligibility for the alternative route is tightly restricted: no prior offenses, no outstanding warrants, no signs of aggression, and no medical emergencies. If conditions are met, officers contact a specialized dispatch line to initiate a connection with a mobile care unit. That team must arrive within half an hour; if they don’t, arrest becomes the default action. Officers are not permitted to delay or linger while waiting.\n\n***\n\nThe shift reflects a broader reevaluation of how a nation balances compassion with control. While some see this as a pragmatic adjustment, others worry it undermines progress made in addressing root causes. For many, the challenge remains: how to help those struggling without turning every interaction into a legal ordeal. Even simple acts—like eating a meal, shopping for groceries, or speaking with a kind tongue—can become fraught when systems prioritize enforcement over empathy.\n\nP.P.S At this age, in this country, the ability to eat without fear, shop without suspicion, or speak with a gentle tongue feels like a luxury few can count on.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.554117739200592}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -8.904537389753386e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.31348296999931335}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.007826023735105991}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.24009528756141663}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.9124716520309448}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.03640199452638626}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.03628002479672432}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.050031621009111404}, {"token": " reinst", "logprob": -1.1101841926574707}, {"token": "ates", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.5083760619163513}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": " carrying", "logprob": -0.5961156487464905}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.00213300040923059}, {"token": " quantities", "logprob": -0.07987315952777863}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.05112536624073982}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -0.00015937011630740017}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.003980927634984255}, {"token": "entanyl", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0019290901254862547}, {"token": " heroin", "logprob": -9.77468371274881e-05}, {"token": ",", "logprob": 0.0}, {"tok